= Von Staal Cabinet

Chancellor Staal has unveiled his new cabinet—retaining all but two of Goodryke’s ministers. His top priority, he claims, is putting people back to work. To that end, he has appointed Günther Gereke, President of the Rural Municipal Congress and a reformist economic theorist, as <i>Commonwealth Commissioner</i> for Employment, with a full seat in the cabinet.

For now, Staal is enjoying a political honeymoon. The relief at Goodryke’s fall has been widespread, and his public disavowal of any plans to alter the constitution has calmed both the national mood and the <span style="color: #000000;">repu</span><span style="color: #DD0000;">bli</span><span style="color: #FFCC00;">cans</span> who had bitterly opposed his predecessor.

The <span style="color: #000000;">Christian</span> trade unions and their affiliated parties have adopted a conciliatory stance, while the ADGB and <span style="color: #c00000;">**Marchers**</span> leaderships, viewing Staal as the lesser evil, have responded positively to his public works proposals.

But the real issue isn’t popular unrest—it’s whether Staal can survive the no-confidence vote looming in the Assembly later this week.